# HeritageGraph AI — environment template
#
# The backend reads process environment ONLY (no dotenv dependency): copy
# the values you need into your shell / process manager, or use this file
# as documentation. NEVER put real credentials in a committed file — set
# them in your shell, CI secrets or deployment secret store.
#
# Copy:  cp .env.example .env   (then keep .env out of version control)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- core ---
# Required. SQLAlchemy URL. SQLite for development, PostgreSQL for production.
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///heritage.db
# ENVIRONMENT=development          # development | test | production
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO                   # DEBUG .. CRITICAL
# LOG_JSON=false                   # defaults to true when ENVIRONMENT=production
# API_PREFIX=/api

# ------------------------------------------------------ image analysis ---
# Vision is MANDATORY for live analysis: it turns the image into entities.
# Any OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint works; no default is invented.
VISION_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com/v1
VISION_API_KEY=<your-vision-key>
VISION_MODEL=<model-name-you-purchased>
# VISION_TIMEOUT=120               # seconds, default 120, max 600
#
# Verified working example (real calls, free tier, no card) — Google
# Gemini's OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The model below is the one this
# repository's live runs used (real uploads analysed end to end):
#   VISION_API_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/
#   VISION_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# Model ids churn and not every id in the catalog serves image input — list
# what your key may use (GET {VISION_API_BASE_URL}models) and probe it:
#   cd backend && python -m scripts.vision_smoke <image> [--ocr]

# ------------------------------------------------------------- OCR (optional) --
# OCR is OPTIONAL: without it extraction runs vision-only and the run carries an
# explicit "OCR skipped" warning (the analysis can still complete when the vision
# model read *some* text; it is marked `partial` when neither provider produced
# text, never `completed` with an invented inscription).
#
# Provider selection: OCR_PROVIDER=ocr_space | google_vision | http  (unset => http)
#
# 1) OCR.space (RECOMMENDED) — a real OCR API with a free key issued
#    instantly at https://ocr.space/ocrapi/freekey (no billing, no approval).
#    It is a plain HTTP call, so nothing else has to be installed.
#
#      OCR_PROVIDER=ocr_space
#      OCR_SPACE_API_KEY=<your-ocr-space-key>
#      # OCR_SPACE_API_URL=https://api.ocr.space/parse/image   # official default
#      # OCR_SPACE_LANGUAGE=auto                              # default; not English-only
#      # OCR_SPACE_ENGINE=2                                   # 1 | 2 | 3 (default 2)
#      # OCR_SPACE_MAX_BYTES=1000000                          # free-plan file limit
#
#    How to run a real OCR test (prints the recognised text; it reads
#    backend/.env automatically, so nothing has to be exported by hand):
#      cd backend && .venv/Scripts/python -m scripts.vision_smoke <image.png> --ocr-only
#    Free plan limits (from the official docs): 1 MB per file, 500 requests
#    per day per IP, 25,000 per month; PNG/JPG/GIF/TIF/BMP (+PDF). WebP and
#    AVIF uploads are transcoded automatically; anything over 1 MB is
#    recompressed before it is sent (quality first, resolution only as much
#    as needed to stay legible).
#    The key is read by the backend ONLY — never put it in the frontend or
#    in VITE_* variables, which are public to the browser.
#
# 2) Google Cloud Vision — alternative provider (needs a GCP project).
#    Uses the documented REST `files:annotate` operation
#    (DOCUMENT_TEXT_DETECTION, which also covers plain photographs) with
#    language hints, and merges multi-region results in reading order.
#    Auth (in precedence order, first one present wins):
#      GOOGLE_VISION_ACCESS_TOKEN  short-lived OAuth2 token (workload identity,
#                                  `gcloud auth print-access-token` in dev)
#      GOOGLE_VISION_API_KEY       API key for the Vision API
#      GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/abs/path/service-account.json
#                                  service-account key; the adapter mints a
#                                  token itself (google-auth is installed)
#    GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT is only used to scope the quota project for
#    user-credential flows; it is not required for a service account.
#    In production prefer workload/managed identity or the platform secret
#    store over a committed key file. NEVER commit the JSON key.
# OCR_PROVIDER=google_vision
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/abs/path/to/service-account.json
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id
# GOOGLE_VISION_API_KEY=<your-vision-api-key>          # alternative to the JSON key
# GOOGLE_VISION_ENDPOINT=https://vision.googleapis.com/v1/images:annotate
#
# 3) Generic HTTP OCR — POST multipart (field `image`) + Bearer key; accepts
#    JSON {"text": ...} or text/plain. Set both variables or neither.
# OCR_PROVIDER=http
# OCR_API_URL=https://ocr.example.com/v1/read
# OCR_API_KEY=<your-ocr-key>
#
# OCR_TIMEOUT=30                   # seconds, default 30, max 600 (all providers)
#
# OCR is optional: with no provider configured the analysis runs vision-only
# and carries an explicit "OCR skipped" warning naming the variable for the
# provider you selected (OCR_SPACE_API_KEY for OCR.space).

# --------------------------------------------------- historical sources --
# Live retrieval against Wikipedia's public search API (no key required).
# Recommended for real demos: query-dependent, citable records with URLs.
# Degrades to the local seeded store when disabled or unreachable.
# Also supplies the subject photograph, related-topic thumbnails and their
# canonical types (Wikimedia + Wikidata, both public and key-free).
# HG_LIVE_SOURCES=1
# HG_SOURCE_TIMEOUT=8              # seconds, default 8
# Transient-failure retries per source request (HTTP 429, 5xx, timeouts).
# Default 2 attempts (the first try plus one retry), clamped to 1..5. The
# retry is bounded by a 10 s wall-clock budget per call, so a hanging source
# is never paid for twice; permanent 4xx answers are never retried.
# HG_SOURCE_RETRIES=2
#
# Wikimedia throttles clients whose User-Agent has no contact details:
# verified live, the bare product name answered HTTP 429 while the same
# request carrying a URL/email answered 200 immediately. The default uses
# APP_ORIGIN as the contact, so set your own project URL or address for a
# public deployment (this is also what keeps live search from degrading).
# HG_SOURCE_USER_AGENT=HeritageGraphAI/1.0 (+https://your-site.example; you@example.org)

# --------------------------------------------------- Adaption synthesis ---
# OPTIONAL. Adaption Labs is wired in as the only model allowed to *write* a
# summary: it may compose one short paragraph over the briefing's quoted
# statements, and every sentence must cite them or the summary is dropped.
# It is NOT a vision provider (its docs do not document image input), so
# image understanding stays with VISION_* above.
#
# Status for this deployment (verified with scripts/probe_adaption.py):
#   * the key authenticates (GET /models -> 200)
#   * the hosted inference catalog is EMPTY for this account
#     (GET /models?type=hosted -> {"data":[]}) and POST /responses answers
#     404 "Model not found" for every model id probed
#   => synthesis is inert here until a hosted model is provisioned.
#
# ADAPTION_API_KEY=<your-adaption-key>   # or put it in backend/.adaption_key
# ADAPTION_MODEL=<hosted-model-id>       # from GET /models?type=hosted
# ADAPTION_API_BASE_URL=https://api.prod.adaptionlabs.ai/api/v1
# ADAPTION_TIMEOUT=30                    # seconds, default 30
# HG_SYNTHESIS=1                         # opt in; off => verbatim-quote briefings only
#
# Probe the provider without starting the app:
#   cd backend && python -m scripts.probe_adaption --auth --models

# ------------------------------------------------ authentication/session --
# Email + password accounts. Passwords are stored as PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256
# (600k iterations, per-user salt); sessions are opaque 256-bit tokens stored
# as SHA-256 digests and carried in an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie
# (`hg_session`, Secure when the request is HTTPS). No signing secret is
# needed — nothing is trusted from the client except the opaque token.
#
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=168            # 7 days, clamped to 1 hour .. 30 days
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1            # only behind a proxy that SETS X-Forwarded-Proto
#                                  # (otherwise a client could claim https and
#                                  #  receive a Secure cookie over plain HTTP)
#
# The app is same-origin by design (Vite proxies /api), so no CORS is needed.
# Only if you serve the frontend from another origin, list it explicitly —
# `*` is rejected because credentialed requests cannot use wildcards:
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.example.org

# ------------------------------------------- heritage archive studio -----
# Uploaded originals are preserved on disk, outside the database, referenced
# by an opaque storage key. Extracted text lives in the database. The default
# root is backend/var/archive (git-ignored); point it at a mounted volume or
# an object-storage sync target in deployment.
# HG_STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/heritagegraph/archive
#
# Per-file limits are enforced before anything is read into memory:
#   25 MB per accepted file, 100 MB per ZIP upload, 20 files per request,
#   150 MB per multipart request. ZIP handling is additionally bounded:
#   entries, per-entry bytes, total uncompressed bytes, expansion ratio and
#   nesting depth (nested archives beyond the depth are skipped). Path
#   traversal, absolute paths and executable members are always rejected.
# HG_ZIP_MAX_ENTRIES=60            # members inspected per uploaded ZIP
# HG_ZIP_MAX_ENTRY_BYTES=26214400  # 25 MB per extracted member
# HG_ZIP_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES=262144000 # 250 MB total uncompressed output
# HG_ZIP_MAX_DEPTH=1               # 1 = the uploaded ZIP only, no nesting
#
# The archive pipeline reuses the OCR configuration above; with no OCR
# provider it extracts the document text layer only and marks a scanned file
# `partial` with an explicit reason instead of inventing text.

# ---------------------------------------------------------- demo mode ----
# FIXTURE pipeline for demonstrations without credentials. Results are
# labelled "DEMO MODE" on every run; refused outright when
# ENVIRONMENT=production. Never set this for real analysis.
# HG_DEMO_MODE=1

# ------------------------------------------------- same-origin image proxy --
# Subject photographs and topic thumbnails are served through this backend so
# image display cannot be broken by the browser (shields, DNS filtering,
# corporate proxy) failing to reach a third-party host. Allowlisted Wikimedia
# image hosts only; on by default. MAX 8 MiB per image, images cached 10 min.
# HG_MEDIA_PROXY=off             # load upstream image URLs directly instead
#
# Wikimedia's User-Agent policy requires a descriptive agent WITH a contact URL
# or email, and its CDN answers 403 ("respect our robot policy") without one —
# verified live: the identical request passes once a contact is present. The
# default uses APP_ORIGIN (or http://localhost:8000) as the contact; set your
# own project URL or address here, especially for a public deployment.
# HG_MEDIA_USER_AGENT=HeritageGraphAI/0.1 (+https://your-site.example; you@example.org)
#
# APP_ORIGIN is the deployment's public origin; the Sites platform injects it.
# It is used as the proxy's default contact and (in that deployment) as the
# public address reported by the app.
# APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8000

# ------------------------------------------------------ stories & legends --
# Community oral histories are accepted from signed-in users and stored
# `pending`. Publishing one requires an account whose email is on this
# allowlist (comma or semicolon separated). Empty by default: submissions are
# collected for review but nothing becomes public until a deployment names its
# moderators. Approval labels the record a community account, never verified
# history.
# STORY_MODERATOR_EMAILS=curator@example.org,archivist@example.org

# ------------------------------------------------------------- frontend --
# Only needed for standalone builds/tests; the Vite dev server proxies /api.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
#
# One-command dev server (`npm run dev`) — all optional:
#   HG_BACKEND_ORIGIN    what the dev server proxies /api to (set automatically
#                        by scripts/dev.mjs to the backend's actual port)
#   VITE_DEV_HOST        bind address (127.0.0.1). Use 0.0.0.0 to open the app
#                        from another device/browser; development only, no auth
#   VITE_DEV_PORT        dev port (5173)
#   VITE_PREVIEW_PORT    `npm run preview` port (4173)
#   VITE_ALLOWED_HOSTS   extra Host values Vite accepts, comma separated, or *
#                        (Vite answers 403 "Blocked request" for unknown hosts,
#                        which is what breaks access via a tunnel/machine name)
#   VITE_MEDIA_PROXY=off load images directly instead of via /api/media

# ---------------------------------------------- gated live test runs -----
# Opt-in real-network tests (each also requires the relevant config above):
# HERITAGE_LIVE_PROVIDER_TESTS=1   # real OCR/vision provider calls
# HERITAGE_LIVE_SOURCE_TESTS=1     # real MediaWiki API query